    "origin_processing_flavor_boundary_v1": "The evidence supports real possible flavor variation without making an origin label deterministic. Dark chocolate can show several flavors, with variation especially noticeable among single-origin products. Flavor cocoa may come from a region, crop or variety or use specific fermentation and harvesting techniques; its assessments include genetics, acidity, fermentation, drying, disease, mold and flavor and combine objective and subjective judgments. Single-origin can cover anything from a country to a plantation and does not necessarily mean one bean type. Fermentation develops flavor precursors later expressed during roasting and reduces bitterness. Traditional Forastero, Criollo and Trinitario categories remain common in marketing but are no longer treated as having a botanical basis. Origin, percentage, genotype, growing conditions, fermentation, drying, roasting, recipe and processing can matter, but no label alone predicts this recipient's flavor ranking.",
    "truffle_format_recipient_fit_boundary_v1": "A traditional chocolate truffle has a ganache center and a coating, and named styles can use cream, butter, dark or milk chocolate, condensed milk, liqueur, buttercream, nut paste, milk powder or other fats. Therefore a truffle-box piece is not equivalent to an unfilled dark bar segment, and total box mass or piece count does not reveal its share of recipient-acceptable dark chocolate. For both exact boxes, verify piece allocation, shell type and percentage, filling, ingredient, allergen, alcohol, freshness and storage information before comparing them with plain bars for a recipient who normally keeps 85-percent bars.",
